  'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>Finding a summer apartment for students in Houston comes down to three things: picking a neighborhood that keeps your commute short, finding a landlord who will do a short lease and starting early. Whether you are taking summer classes, interning downtown or staying in town over break, this guide covers where to look, what to watch for and how to move in smoothly.</p>

<h2>Why Houston Works Well for a Summer Stay</h2>
<p>Houston is home to the University of Houston, Rice University, Texas Southern University and many other schools, so there is a large student population and plenty of housing aimed at it. The city also offers museums, parks and a huge food scene to fill your free time.</p>
<p>Getting around without a car is possible in parts of town. <a href="https://www.ridemetro.org/">METRO Houston</a> runs buses and light rail connecting downtown, Midtown, the Museum District, the Medical Center and areas near UH. Check routes from any apartment you are considering to your campus or internship, and ask about student fare options.</p>

<h2>Neighborhoods Students Often Choose</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Third Ward:</strong> next to UH and Texas Southern, often with more budget-friendly options and short trips to campus.</li>
<li><strong>Montrose:</strong> walkable, artsy and full of coffee shops and restaurants.</li>
<li><strong>Midtown:</strong> a good in-between spot if you are interning downtown, with lots of dining and nightlife.</li>
<li><strong>Museum District:</strong> quieter, close to Rice and Hermann Park, with plenty of green space.</li>
</ul>
<p>Houston summers are hot, so a short walk or transit ride matters more than it seems. A place ten minutes closer can make a real difference in July.</p>

<h2>Where to Search for Short-Term Housing</h2>
<h3>Local listing sites</h3>
<p><a href="https://houstonapartments.com/">HoustonApartments.com</a> focuses on the Houston market and lets you filter by price, pet policy and other details. It is a good place to start your search and to compare neighborhoods quickly.</p>
<h3>Your school's housing office</h3>
<p>Many universities offer summer housing for enrolled students or keep off-campus listings and roommate boards. Check your school's housing portal before you commit elsewhere.</p>
<h3>Sublets</h3>
<p>Plenty of students leave town for the summer and want someone to take over their room. University groups and housing boards are common places to find these. Make sure the original tenant is allowed to sublet under their lease.</p>

<h3>Help with housing costs</h3>
<p>If cost is a serious concern, look into local programs. The <a href="https://housingforhouston.com/">Houston Housing Authority</a> provides information on affordable housing and rental assistance. Most programs are aimed at longer-term residents and have eligibility rules, and many have waiting lists, so they are rarely a quick fix for a summer stay. Still, independent students or students supporting a family should check what they may qualify for. Your school's financial aid office can also point you to emergency funds or housing help.</p>

<h2>Tips for Landing the Right Summer Place</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Start early.</strong> Begin looking two to three months ahead.</li>
<li><strong>Be clear about lease length.</strong> Ask specifically for short-term or month-to-month terms, and expect to pay more per month for flexibility.</li>
<li><strong>Consider furnished options.</strong> Moving furniture in and out for a few months rarely makes sense.</li>
<li><strong>Know what is included.</strong> Compare whether utilities, internet and laundry are part of the rent.</li>
<li><strong>Think about roommates.</strong> Splitting a two-bedroom can cut your cost significantly.</li>
</ol>

<div class="callout"><p><strong>Pro tip:</strong> Never send money for a sublet you have not seen in person or on a live video call, and be wary of anyone who pushes for a wire transfer or gift cards. If the deal seems too good for the neighborhood, it probably is.</p></div>

<h3>Budgeting for a summer lease</h3>
<p>Short stays come with costs that a regular lease spreads out. Build a simple budget before you commit:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Rent,</strong> which may be higher per month for short terms.</li>
<li><strong>Deposit and fees,</strong> including application, admin and any short-term premium.</li>
<li><strong>Utilities and internet,</strong> especially electricity, since AC runs hard in a Houston summer.</li>
<li><strong>Transportation,</strong> whether that is METRO fares, parking or fuel.</li>
<li><strong>Furniture rental or basics,</strong> if the unit is not furnished.</li>
</ul>
<p>Electricity deserves special attention. Ask the leasing office or the current tenant what summer bills typically run for the unit, and whether the AC has been serviced recently.</p>
<p>Then compare that total with on-campus summer housing or a sublet. Sometimes a slightly pricier option with utilities and furniture included is the cheaper choice overall.</p>

<h2>Making the Move Smooth</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Tour the unit,</strong> in person or by live video, and test the AC.</li>
<li><strong>Read the lease</strong> for early termination, deposits, fees and utilities.</li>
<li><strong>Check the area</strong> at different times of day, and look at local crime information from the city.</li>
<li><strong>Know your rights</strong> as a Texas tenant; the Texas Attorney General's office publishes renter information.</li>
<li><strong>Document move-in condition</strong> with photos so you get your deposit back.</li>
</ul>
<p>Pack light. A few sturdy [[amz:moving_boxes|moving boxes]] and some damage-free hooks are usually all you need. Summer sun comes up early, and [[amz:blackout_curtains|blackout curtains]] help if you are studying late or working odd hours.</p>
